# Icons in Swaybar

While at work on a fresh install the same settings defaulted to nice looking Swaybar icons, at home they were coloured and awkward. I fixed this this morning with a combination of:

* An explicit `font` declaration.
* Replacing emoji usage with icon unicode points.

Here is the `bar` section of my Sway config:

```
bar {
    position top
    font pango:SourceCodePro Regular 9

    status_command while /home/colin/dotfiles/sway/status.sh; do sleep 5; done

    colors {
        statusline #ffffff
        background #323232
        inactive_workspace #323232 #323232 #5c5c5c
    }
}
```

And the status script:

```
#!/usr/bin/env sh

date_formatted=$(date +"%Y-%m-%d %R")

battery_status=$(cat /sys/class/power_supply/BAT0/capacity)%

if ip link | rg enp0s31f6 | rg "state UP" >/dev/null; then
	eth_status="$(printf '\uef44')"
else
	eth_status=""
fi

if ip link | rg wlp4s0 | rg "state UP" >/dev/null; then
	ssid="$(iwgetid -r) $(printf '\uf1eb')"
else
	ssid=""
fi

echo "$eth_status $ssid $battery_status $(printf '\uf240') $date_formatted $(printf '\uf073')"
```

The key for nice icons are the `$(printf '\uf1eb')` parts. The codes themselves can be looked up manually via the Nerd Font Cheatsheet:

=> https://www.nerdfonts.com/cheat-sheet

I used to have a more involved status bar, but recently reverted back to vanilla `sway-bar`. It looks nice, it works, and moreover I understand it. With enough languages, tools, configuration rules, and project contexts packed into my head, the ability to "recontextualize" effectively after being away from a particular project or file for a long time is critical. Using tools that are a simple as possible is becoming more and more important for me.

